// @proven: 0
// @failed: 0
// @unknown: 1
// The soundness guard on pureCallSharedValue. Both calls are written `double(v)`, but `v`
// changed between them, so they must NOT collapse to one symbol — the real answer is -8.
//
// What this fixture pins is the `proven: 0`. Sharing is keyed on the lowered ARGUMENT
// TERMS rather than the call's text, and the reassignment renames `v`, so the two calls
// key differently and nothing here is provable. If sharing ever keyed on the call site
// instead, `a - b == 0` would come back PROVEN — a false proof — and this goes red.
//
// It lands on `unknown` rather than `failed` because `double` declares no `ensures`, so
// its value is invented: any counterexample would name a return the callee cannot produce.
// That degradation is deliberate (see `opaqueCalls` in verify.ts) and it suppresses
// refutations only, never proofs — which is exactly what makes this fixture still bite.
@pure
fn double(x: i64): i64 {
    return x * 2
}

pub fn drift(): i64
ensures result == 0
{
    var v = 1
    let a = double(v)
    v = 5
    let b = double(v)
    return a - b
}
